WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:12.630
Good afternoon everybody.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.920
Today, we have Alex Schultz,
VP of growth at Facebook, and

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.290
probably the world expert on growth,
as he can vouch from this weekend,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.720
the person I go to when I have
a really hard growth question.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.030
Many people who work at Facebook, many of
these founders at Facebook in fact, and

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.540
very early employees have said hiring
Alex has been one of the secret

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.880
successes of Facebook that
is not well understood.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.940
You joined almost ten years ago, and

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.950
I think at that point Facebook
had less than 100 million users?

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Way less than 100 million users.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.140
Facebook now has 1.6 billion and
Alex has been after the importance

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.830
of having a great product, maybe
the second most important driver of that.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.120
&gt;&gt; Having Naomi,
these very important people.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.967
&gt;&gt; And the team, anyway, thank you
very much for coming to speak to us.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:56.496
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:56.496 --> 00:01:01.222
I did not pay Sam for that introduction
and I would note that Havi is my boss so,

00:01:01.222 --> 00:01:01.856
hi Havi.

00:01:01.856 --> 00:01:04.230
But no, Havi and Naomi, Danny,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.590
they are these people who've been in
the growth team for four years and

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:14.700
then people like Blake, and Martin, and
Ray and James Wang who set the team up.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.840
And it's very nice, but I am a VP on
growth, I am not the VP of growth.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.370
So you've had an amazing lecture series.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.550
I just watched all of them yesterday to
make sure I didn't say anything without

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.330
actually having seen
what everybody else said.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.000
And it was totally awesome.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.980
I loved what D'Angelo had to say, so
that lecture, if you weren't here, if you

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.490
didn't watch it, I strongly recommend you
go back and see what Adam D'Angelo said,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.780
because I could not emphasize his
points on metrics more than enough.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.820
And so what I'm going to try and do for

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.740
the first half is go over some of
the points that have been made already and

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.610
emphasize what I think is most important
when you're thinking about growth and

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.710
what my take is and our take is on
the Facebook growth team on those.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.340
And then in the second half, try and
talk to you a little bit more about

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.670
tactics that have been
particularly useful.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.010
The caveat that I think you should take
onboard is what Stewart from Slack and

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.010
Flickr said.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:11.700
Which is to some extent, you do have 100
million monkeys bashing at typewriters,

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.670
and I am very, very lucky to be
on the growth team at Facebook

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.970
to get to work on a product like Facebook
that has the retention that Facebook does.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.990
That has everyone in the world wanting
to use the product and to say that,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:27.240
I drove it or any of us drove it beyond
their being great product market fit

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.420
is perhaps not true.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.030
I can't tell you what the AB test
is if we didn't even have a growth

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.840
team at Facebook.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.640
So what matters most to growth?

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.180
The single most important
thing to growth is retention.

00:02:45.056 --> 00:02:50.300
Adam commented on this in his talk, and he
showed you cohorts from some real company.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.460
But here is a stylized cohort of
how you should look at retention.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.830
The number of days from
acquisition is on the x axis.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.320
On the y axis is the percent of
people who has still monthly active.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.220
I care a lot about using monthly active,
not daily active, not weekly active.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.824
There are really good arguments for using
daily active that people say all the time.

00:03:08.824 --> 00:03:11.185
Because it's about intensity of usage.

00:03:11.185 --> 00:03:13.769
But what really, really matters is
that you got skin in the game and

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:16.310
people come back to your
product at least once a month.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.640
And then you build on top
of that engagement revenue,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.800
whatever your key metrics are which
we'll get to in a minute, but firstly,

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.890
I believe monthly active is
the most important thing.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:30.100
The second thing is how do
you rally calculate it?

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.550
So I've got a real graph for
revenue in a few slides from Facebook from

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.370
the first 250 days of our ads products,
which I'll show you.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.130
But the thing that's really,
really important is to say that,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:46.260
it's very easy to calculate this curve
even if you have 1,000 or 100 users.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.220
And what you do is you look at all
of your users on the first day

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.450
after they signed up.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:53.379
Were they monthly active?

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:55.920
Yes, clearly, right?

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.430
The first 30 days everyone's
going to be monthly active.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.600
I'd prefer if you defined a month as 28
days, by the way, because multiples of

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.740
seven is really, really good for
these kind of metrics because of weeks.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.120
But for the first 30 days, everyone
has been active on their first day,

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.860
on their second day, on their third
day as a monthly active user,

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:11.850
whether they came back or not.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.430
And then what you do
is you look at day 31.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.850
And obviously, only some of your users
will have been active for 31 days.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.139
Everyone who signed up a day
ago was not active for 31 days.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.555
So what percentage of them
were active on day 31,

00:04:26.555 --> 00:04:29.631
what percentage of them
were active on day 32?

00:04:29.631 --> 00:04:33.377
And as you go further and further out in
the number of days since registration,

00:04:33.377 --> 00:04:37.420
the number of people who have been
on your service that long declines.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.090
Which means that your metric is
going to get noisier, right,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.750
as you get out to the far
right hand side of that curve.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.010
But that doesn't mean you can't get
a handle of where things flatten out very,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.380
very early on with very
small numbers of users.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.940
The number one criticism people give me
when I talk about retention as a metric.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.970
Or even when you look at what Adam
presented in his presentation earlier is

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.360
you need lots and lots of users.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.550
So you need millions of users to
calculate that, that is not true.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.350
We did it with thousands of users in the
early days of advertisers for Facebook for

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:10.870
example.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:16.910
Or you need to have had all of those
users on for a very long time.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:20.320
Look at Adam's graph it was multiple years
with months, and months, and months.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.250
No, you can do it day by day, and

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.840
you can get to a really good place super,
super fast, using this methodology.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.260
So what you're looking for
in a retention curve?

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.310
You're looking for it to flat line above
zero, not flat line like a heart monitor.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.325
You want it to flat line at some line
that asymptotes with the x-axis.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.680
There are three types of
these curves that I've seen.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.190
The first type is where
asymptotes to the x-axis.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.360
Like really, really close,
goes down to almost zero.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.770
You hear about a lot of those companies.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.880
You think about most games,
most games are smash hits.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.160
How many people use FarmVille now versus
how many people used FarmVille at its

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.895
absolute, please tell me
you know what FarmVille is.

00:06:01.895 --> 00:06:04.060
[LAUGH] Yeah, okay, all right.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.900
How many people used farm,
I'm feeling old.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.850
I had hair when I first did one of these.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:11.280
How many people use FarmVille now
versus how many people used to use it?

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.210
FarmVille was a smash hit, then there's
other ones which look like this,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.380
where they stabilize out.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.910
So you think about Minecraft,
right, or World of Warcraft.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.690
These games that somehow have network
effects and people stay in them for

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:24.250
a long time.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.180
The other version for this curve I've seen
is the ones where they go down, they look

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:32.050
like they're going to hit the x-axis and
then they re-accelerate and they go up.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.180
There's a few different things that can
cause that right, so you have this,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.040
this overwhelming thing that is driving
you down to have zero retention and

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.190
somehow the numbers go up again.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.340
So things that drive that for
businesses, and

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.216
you actually I think
mentioned this in your intro.

00:06:46.216 --> 00:06:50.600
One, network effects, so

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.010
when all your friends get on Facebook,
you're much more likely to use Facebook.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.560
When all your friends get on Twitter,

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.100
you'll more likely use the Twitter,
WhatsApp, Messenger.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.950
Whichever social service
you're talking about,

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:03.300
there are network effects
that really matter.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.280
Network effects also
count in marketplaces.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.050
When you have enough sellers,
you can have enough buyers.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.430
When you have enough traffic,
you get more sellers.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:15.990
And so as marketplaces go, often what you
find is these old cohorts that signed up

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.190
resurrect and come back to the service.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:21.800
So network effects can drive the curve
up after it's flatlined somewhere or

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:22.600
is heading towards zero.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.800
The second thing that can do this
is opening up a new interface.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:29.810
So think about it.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.860
A lot of people release
their apps first on iOS.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.600
I actually loved Diane's talk last week
for this, by the point that he made of

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.190
starting with Nokia, focusing on Android
because it's what most of the world use,

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:46.270
but in Silicon Valley we typically build
our apps first on iOS more often than not.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.691
And then when someone releases
an Android application, and

00:07:49.691 --> 00:07:53.122
this was something I saw with
Instagram years and years ago.

00:07:53.122 --> 00:07:54.958
But when you release
an Android application,

00:07:54.958 --> 00:07:57.990
people might have tried it on an iPhone
And then traded their iPhone for

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.080
an Android and
no longer be able to access your service.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.670
People might've tried your
service on a friend's iPhone and

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.380
then when you add an Android they
come back and use your service.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.600
So that's another thing that
can drive you up again.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.480
And then the third thing that I've seen
on this curves that drive this curves up

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.750
again is adding categories,
so think about Amazon.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.200
There was a really good discussion,
was that Stuart earlier or?

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.570
Someone earlier in this talked about
Amazon, and how Amazon started with books

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:26.350
because there was this large
inventory available of books.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.260
But then they added in another category.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.210
And then they added in
another category over time.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:37.190
And imagine, there will be people who came
to Amazon to buy that one book, but then

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.830
two years down the line Amazon suddenly
had kitchenware, I don't know, or fashion.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:42.940
And they came back and
then they bought fashion and

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.170
they brought them more use cases
to come back more monthly active.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.170
This was true with eBay.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:48.980
We added motors.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:52.980
I remember being at eBay and
we had to do all of our reporting on

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.240
gross merchandise volume with and
without motors.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.190
And no one had ever believed that you
could buy a car online, and eBay did it.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.800
And it created a whole new use case for
people, many of whom were existing eBay

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.040
customers, who now had one more
category they could shop in.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.500
So you want to curve the asymptotes
to a line parallel to the x-axis.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:15.430
You can do it with far more
limited amounts of data

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:19.100
than most people think you can by using
the technique I outlined earlier, and

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.910
I'll show you a real world
example in a few slides.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.570
And there are three types
of curves that I've seen.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:29.520
Curves that asymptote like this,
awesome, your company will survive.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:30.330
And how big will it get?

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:31.150
We have another question.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.000
Curves to go straight to zero.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.900
That's okay, you could be in games and

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.940
your job is to produce one hit
after another after another.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.168
EA doesn't expect whatever fee for 2012,

00:09:41.168 --> 00:09:44.240
they still have lots of users
now if they've released FIFA 27.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.300
So it's okay, there are businesses
where hitting the x axis is okay.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.110
But you need to know you're
in one of those businesses.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.150
And then the third that I've
seen is the curve going up.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.280
And that can be driven by network effects.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.110
That can be driven by being
available on more platforms.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:02.850
That can be driven by adding categories
to your marketplace or service.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:07.340
From this falls out a brilliant
way to look at growth, and

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.160
this guy Danny Ferrante who was at Yahoo.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.870
Who was one of the first members of
the growth team runs core data science at

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.490
Facebook, he's totally awesome.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:17.110
He's really the brains behind
the operation I'm the mouth.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.490
Danny came up or brought this to Facebook,
and it's a simple way to look at growth.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:26.520
We were looking at growth as how many
users were we growing by week on week?

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:29.090
We looked at it on an absolute basis.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.310
And looking at it like
that we were like okay so

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:33.850
we're signing up whatever
it was a million a week and

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.737
we're growing by whatever it was
800,000 a week I don't know.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.330
Cool, we're churning 200,000 people.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:42.030
And we never actually
looked at it in this way.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.850
When we change the way we look
at things to say well okay,

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:50.710
the net growth is 800,000 a week,
the new users are 1 million a week.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:54.680
But what is churn and resurrection?

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.500
And what we actually found at the time,
and

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.000
I can't remember the absolute numbers so
I'll just make some up.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.395
But imagine you're churning 1.3 million.

00:11:02.395 --> 00:11:05.012
So not 200,000, 1.3 million.

00:11:05.012 --> 00:11:07.630
You're resurrecting 1.1 million.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:11.355
So plus 1 minus 1.3 plus 1.1,

00:11:11.355 --> 00:11:15.811
you've got net 800,000 in growth.

00:11:15.811 --> 00:11:18.736
But that 800,000 in
growth is being driven,

00:11:18.736 --> 00:11:22.988
I think I did the math wrong, but
800,000 in growth is being driven by

00:11:22.988 --> 00:11:28.090
a different order of magnitude from churn
and resurrection than you realized.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:31.020
And so that helps you focus on
how do I stop people churning?

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.190
How do I get people to come back to my
service who are not coming back to my

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:37.740
service right now?

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.960
So once you have your retention curves,
and

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.390
once you start to have
a business with retention.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.850
You need to look at
your growth accounting,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.710
not just how many users are we acquiring.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:48.290
Make sense?

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.030
Overall the number one thing
I focus on for growth,

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.600
and the one thing that we focus on for
growth is retention.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.796
So what gets you retained
in any given service?

00:12:00.336 --> 00:12:02.290
Magic Moment.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:05.240
What is the moment when you used Airbnb?

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.570
What is the moment when used Door Dash,
when you used Slack,

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:11.390
when you used Facebook,
LinkedIn, WhatsApp?

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:15.870
Whatever the product, what is that
moment when you went, yes, I've got it.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.520
Right, on Facebook it's friends.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:18.730
You see that first friend,

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:23.670
that person who I've got completely
the wrong crowd for this but the person

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:27.170
from high school who you lost contact
with as you grow older and moved away.

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.430
But you see that person and you go, wow.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.340
I haven't talked to them in years.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.720
Let's see what's going on in their life.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.088
And they've had a kid and lost their hair
too, and you feel better about yourself.

00:12:39.176 --> 00:12:41.160
That is a magic moment on Facebook.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.730
So how quickly can you drive
the user to that magic moment.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:45.570
You look at Airbnb, right?

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:50.130
They talked about the delight
that they give people.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:53.050
They talked about these different
screenshots of the stages of the user

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:56.650
journey that they've created
as a map of the user journey.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:59.310
Getting that first person
reserve your house.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.200
Staying at that first property,
those are our magic moments.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:06.440
eBay, buying your first item
from a stranger online.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.960
Nowadays that doesn't seem crazy but
back when I was working at eBay in the UK,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.279
that was revolutionary.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.210
And people really, their eyes lit
up when you talk to them about it.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.530
In Germany they had this great ad, tights
by minds that showed the build up of when

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:23.240
you are waiting for the auction to finish
and view to make your first purchase.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:25.750
That was such an adrenaline
endorphin moment.

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:28.340
But users loved it.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.510
Nowadays, it seems a long time ago.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.160
Those magic moments are what
you need to look for.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:34.730
So how do you find them?

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.460
Well you can find them in
the non scalable way which is

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:39.280
don't they just sound relatively obvious?

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.700
You can actually think through what
is the experience on my product?

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:44.880
What do I want people to do?

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:49.760
You can ask your first users, okay,
you're keeping using my product.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:50.930
Why are you keeping using my,

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.920
what is the thing that makes
you happy to use my product?

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:55.820
What was that first moment?

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:57.740
It's non scalable but it generally works.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.030
When you ask people what is
the best thing about Facebook?

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.150
The first thing they say is
connecting with friends and family.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:06.610
In all the research,
why do you like Facebook?

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.270
It helps me connect to my friends and
family.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.270
So you can do it the non-scalable way or
you can do it the scalable way.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.360
Look for correlations.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:16.780
So Marcus talked at
startup school himself and

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:22.565
he said that we focused on getting
people to ten friends in 14 days.

00:14:22.565 --> 00:14:24.165
It's a smooth curve, right?

00:14:24.165 --> 00:14:28.255
Number of friends on the x axis and
percent retained on the y axis.

00:14:28.255 --> 00:14:31.875
It's not like there's a step change at
ten, if you get nine you don't retain.

00:14:31.875 --> 00:14:33.725
If you get 11 you do and
it all changes at ten.

00:14:33.725 --> 00:14:36.495
No, it's clearly a smooth curve and
we picked a point on the curve.

00:14:36.495 --> 00:14:39.615
But there's a strong correlation between

00:14:39.615 --> 00:14:43.145
number of friends you've got in the first
14 days and whether you retain or not.

00:14:43.145 --> 00:14:45.870
There's a correlation between number
of friends in the first day but

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.010
there are correlations for
any product like did you place a bid,

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:51.920
did you do a buy it now or
did you list an item on eBay?

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:56.300
Was the thing that was completely
correlated with retention for eBay.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.380
You can imagine, I don't know, I mean
DoorDash was founded at Stanford, right?

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.220
You could imagine for DoorDash.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.220
You've placed your first order and
that order arrives to your door.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.910
And whether you place an order or not and
how many orders you place you place in

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:13.300
the first week, probably they are actually
correlated with whether you retain or not.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.590
So you need to look for the magic moment
of your product by talking to users and

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:19.588
then try and
validate that with correlations.

00:15:19.588 --> 00:15:23.302
Makes sense?

00:15:23.302 --> 00:15:25.770
Cool, I love this.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:29.210
I put it in every presentation I could.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:33.900
So I'm lucky enough to have been trained
in physics at Cambridge in the UK.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:39.425
And my favorite and my favorite lecture
series, not my favorite lecture but

00:15:39.425 --> 00:15:42.635
my favorite lecture series was in this
thing called dimensional reasoning.

00:15:42.635 --> 00:15:44.935
And so it's easy for
me to stand up here and say, okay,

00:15:44.935 --> 00:15:48.335
you need to get flat retention and
then you have a viable business.

00:15:48.335 --> 00:15:51.735
That's not actually true that's
kind of a glib statement.

00:15:51.735 --> 00:15:55.685
If you're producing a social network and
you have 5% retention you're

00:15:55.685 --> 00:15:58.510
not going to long term have
a tremendous social network.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:01.750
Or a messaging app like what use
would WhatsApp be if only 5% of

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:04.410
your friends actually used
WhatsApp who signed up for it.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:05.727
I mean it would be useless, right?

00:16:05.727 --> 00:16:09.441
And there's no way we could release 1
billion plus numbers of active users if

00:16:09.441 --> 00:16:11.140
that was true for us.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:15.440
On the other hand, if you have
a marketplace that is selling fashion.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.320
And you have 5 or 10% retention but

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:22.510
those people spend a lot of money
you have a totally viable business.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.360
And so the level of retention
you need varies dramatically

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:26.960
by the category that you're in.

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.560
Obviously, the higher the better
is true in everything.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:31.480
If you can have an Ebay
type business model or

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:34.540
Amazon type business
model with 90% retention.

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:37.260
You probably end up bigger than Amazon and
that would be incredible.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:42.170
But I think the point I like to make is,
you can figure out and

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:43.660
I love this as an inspirational story.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.460
So Geoffrey Taylor was
this British physicist,

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:48.110
he was on the edge of
the Manhattan Project,

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:51.820
he wasn't in the corp, he ended up winning
a Noble Prize later for a different area.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:54.060
And the US and

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:58.940
Russian governments were releasing these
images to say this is how big our bomb is.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.640
But they weren't releasing the actual
power because that was a closely guarded

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.300
state secret.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.640
And so what Geoffrey Taylor did he
used this technique called dimensional

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:10.400
reasoning where you look at the units in
the metric you were trying to achieve.

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:13.060
So joules, right, for energy, it's joules.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:16.790
Kilograms times meters
squared over seconds squared,

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:19.160
that's what joules decomposes into.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.540
In this case, kilograms,
what are you going to use?

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:25.000
The volume of the air times the density
of the air, which handily, is one.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.433
So you've now got meters cubed times
meters squared over seconds squared.

00:17:29.433 --> 00:17:33.226
So meters [INAUDIBLE] over
seconds times seconds minus 2.

00:17:33.226 --> 00:17:40.510
The time is on here, the diameter,
the radius of the sphere is on here.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:46.730
You just plug the radius in as m,
you plug the diameter of the time in as

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.190
seconds, and you end up getting
25 kilotons as the output.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.320
You get 10 to the 14,
I think is what it works out as.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:58.030
And it turns out the bomb was 21 kilotons
as measured by the US government.

00:17:58.030 --> 00:17:59.490
He could take one photo and

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:02.730
figure out the most closely guarded state
secret in the United States at the time.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.165
You can figure out what retention you
need to have if he can figure that out.

00:18:07.165 --> 00:18:10.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:12.610
here is what I promised,
this is an actual curve.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:14.310
I presented a few different events, so

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:17.610
I taken off the numbers because
I'm worried they're State secrets.

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.540
But this is from 2007 to 2008.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:23.870
I joined the week we started
our ads business at Facebook,

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:26.520
our self-service ad business by us.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:31.190
The pink speaks to the right-hand axis,
the blue speaks to the left-hand axis.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:35.950
What the blue is is how many advertisers
had been in the system at least one day,

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:36.490
two days,

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:40.830
three days because the x-axis is number
of days from acquisition make sense.

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:43.924
So the thing has only
been around 250 days, so

00:18:43.924 --> 00:18:47.102
0 people have been
advertisers 251 days ago.

00:18:47.102 --> 00:18:53.140
And when they turned 51st date and
all the people have been active on day 0.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:58.260
And then each of those pink data points is
the total revenue achieved from every user

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.200
who had registered.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:03.870
Who was using the service
on their 30th day, or

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:08.860
their 31st day divided by how many
users signed up 30 or 31 days ago.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.870
Not how many were active, right?

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:13.120
How many signed up.

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:19.231
So total in the cohort,
who signed up 30 or 31 days ago.

00:19:19.231 --> 00:19:22.920
You divide the revenue they made in
the day by that number of users.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.010
And that gives you that shape
of curve that's up there.

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.000
I stumbled over my words a bit there.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.650
I promise you if you look over
it again it makes perfect sense.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.733
I did this with Danny back in 2008.

00:19:33.733 --> 00:19:37.679
The really important things I want you to
take away from looking at this curve is,

00:19:37.679 --> 00:19:39.200
look how flat the pink line is.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:43.940
Like I've been talking about
curves flattening out,

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.280
this is an actual curve flattening out.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:48.632
And look how quickly it's flattened out.

00:19:48.632 --> 00:19:53.890
By 100 days you can pretty much predict
what the line is going to be at 200 days.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:54.990
I've seen this over and over.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:56.810
I've seen this with engagement.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:20:00.760
So if you divide time spent
by the same denominator.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:02.990
I've seen it actually for sentiment.

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:05.110
If you have large enough
sentiment survey and

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:07.770
you divide it by that same denominator.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.860
I have seen it for posts.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:13.950
I have seen it for
photos uploaded, messages sent.

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:16.600
You can use the same techniques
that I talked about for

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.560
determining how much retention you have
and whether your business is viable,

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:25.670
to figure out what is the lifetime
value of one of your customers.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:29.070
If I was to acquire the total
addressable market for

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:33.550
my business today,
what revenue will I be making in 100 days?

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:37.423
That's what this curve a allows you to do.

00:20:37.423 --> 00:20:39.060
You can predict it for posts.

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:40.740
You can predict it for page views.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.360
Time spent, whatever you want.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:48.510
The same methodology works for
engagement, that works for retention.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:51.410
And, actually, retention is often
the biggest driver of this metric.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.940
And we did this with very little data.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.470
Later on, there's a stat we are okay
releasing, which was end of 2012,

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:01.060
we had about 700,000 active advertisers.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.880
Beginning we had about
300,000 active advertisers.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:08.135
And this was five years before that.

00:21:08.135 --> 00:21:12.130
So you can imagine there was a very small
data set that was behind calculating this.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:15.070
The last thing is I said things
get noisy the further up you go.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:16.963
You can literally see that on this graph,
right?

00:21:16.963 --> 00:21:19.530
The variance in the numbers the further
to the right you go on the graph.

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:22.580
So now, how do you operate for growth?

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:27.160
Most important thing is have a clear goal.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.640
Brian, who is awesome on
growth marketing on Facebook,

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:34.280
great guy, he always says, if you are a
growth team your product is a number.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:39.450
One of the most brilliant things I think
that Mark did in the early days of

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:44.760
Facebook was he aligned the entire company
on monthly active people as our goal.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:46.480
Now why does this matter?

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.910
It matters for a bunch of reasons,
but the biggest and

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:51.580
most important reason is I
think it aligns everyone.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:57.410
When you have a company of more than
two or three people, or by the way,

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:01.200
a team of more than two or three people,
certainly when it gets to the hundreds.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.460
You can't control what anyone does.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.830
I mean, you could probably
control what one person does, but

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:09.750
then the other 199 would do
whatever the hell they wanted.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:13.540
So what do you want to do?

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:16.610
You want to make sure everyone is
pointing in the same direction, and

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:18.860
that direction is generally right.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.360
So let's think back to
the early days of any company.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:25.037
Whether it's Facebook,
whether it's Air BnB, LinkedIn,

00:22:25.037 --> 00:22:26.741
whatever you want to look at.

00:22:26.741 --> 00:22:31.712
It would be completely reasonable
to have a goal of revenue, right?

00:22:31.712 --> 00:22:34.925
You want your company to stay alive,
you want to make enough money that your

00:22:34.925 --> 00:22:39.070
company can still be viable, that you can
employ people, that valuation goes up.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:42.450
So a reasonable person could say,
I think we need to drive towards revenue.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:44.850
At the same time,

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:49.040
you could have a metric that was like,
if you're Air BnB number of hosts.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:50.980
If you're Facebook, number of posts.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:54.190
If your DoorDash number of orders.

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:56.880
I don't even know what this company's
metrics are beyond Facebook's.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.700
You could easily have a production
metric or a consumption metric.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:02.630
You could have a time spent
in our service metric, and

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:06.820
every one of those metrics would be a
completely reasonable metric to drive for.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:10.180
So you've got ten reasonable people,

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:13.070
maybe five of them think you
should have monthly active people.

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:16.410
One is really focused on revenue,
two are looking at time spent and

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:17.810
two are looking at number of posts.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:23.390
Everyone is pushing in a reasonable
direction that is vaguely aligned but

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:25.510
probably will have conflict.

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:28.980
If you want to get ad in front of
users as quickly as you possibly can

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:32.810
you may not necessarily want to put
friends in front of them immediately.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:37.250
If you want to get them to post
you may dial up the composer and

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:38.810
push down the ads.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:40.360
I don't know.
These are trade offs that you could

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:41.360
be making.

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:45.890
And if the whole team isn't clear what
the goal is, they will be in conflict.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.620
So having a clear goal that your
company looks to drive is really,

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:53.200
really important, and

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:58.140
having that goal be MAP in my opinion is
the best metric that you can drive for.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:01.100
Because it is that once
a month visitation,

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:02.638
that base level you can drive off of.

00:24:02.638 --> 00:24:05.330
If you're going to do
a consumer internet company.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:08.410
But fundamentally,
the most important thing is pick a goal,

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:11.270
your team's product is a goal,
focus on that goal.

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:14.250
Align the company around that goal and

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:15.990
then you are going to
operate correctly for growth.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:19.359
The other thing I always say is
you don't need a growth team.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:24.450
If you're a small company, if you're
even series A, don't have a growth team.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:26.200
Don't hire someone like me.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.180
The whole company's job should be growth.

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:32.100
I'm very happy at Facebook,
so I don't want a job offer.

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:36.300
But the whole company's
goal should be growing.

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:39.080
The purpose of a start up is to grow.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.770
So, what is the number you want to grow?

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:43.280
And align the whole company
against growing that number.

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:45.080
Does that make sense?

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.990
Now, Naomi is amazing and Naomi is
someone people don't know that well

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:52.540
out of Facebook, but she runs product and
engineering for growth.

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:55.025
She's brilliant,
she's been at Facebook 12 years.

00:24:55.025 --> 00:24:58.820
So, when you say I'm original,
I feel not original everyday.

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:00.870
She sits like three feet from me,
she's amazing.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:05.050
Anyway, she has this approach that
we use to iterate through our growth

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:07.310
planning cycles,
which I think works really well.

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:11.157
Understand, first of all
understand what is going on.

00:25:11.157 --> 00:25:14.410
Look at all the data you have,
talk to your users.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:17.800
Ask your users what they think.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.960
Understand everything that is going
on with your product that you can

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:21.830
possibly understand.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:22.419
What matters?

00:25:22.419 --> 00:25:23.045
Retention.

00:25:23.045 --> 00:25:24.050
Are you churning too many?

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:25.340
Do you have the retention?

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.450
Is the revenue going up
on a per user basis?

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:29.350
What are the acquisition
channels you're getting?

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:31.010
Which channel are people
resurrecting through?

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:32.090
Are they resurrecting through email?

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:33.120
Is it push notifications?

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:34.270
What's going on?

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:35.750
Understand what's going on for
your system.

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:37.660
Have that all tracked.

00:25:40.068 --> 00:25:45.170
Then identify, look through all of those
different metrics, all of that different

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:50.980
research you get in, and
identify where there are opportunities.

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:52.410
Where do you see big drop-offs?

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:55.240
Do you have a lot of people with
push notifications turned on

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:57.590
who are not receiving push notifications?

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.705
Do you have a lot of people who
are receiving push notifications who

00:26:00.705 --> 00:26:01.938
are not clicking on them?

00:26:01.938 --> 00:26:05.710
Do you have loads and loads of social
media traffic, but no search traffic?

00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:11.570
Are your email viral invites working but
your SMS ones not, and

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.690
what is the difference between those?

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:18.050
Go and look at the data and identify
where there are gaps, and then try and

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:22.310
double down on fixing those
gaps in the execution phase.

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:25.350
Now to this day, this is still
how we run growth at Facebook and

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:31.350
you can even see a a talk Naomi and I
gave online nine years ago I think at FA.

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.450
Eight or nine years ago, where we
talked about using this approach and

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.240
we have kept it going.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:40.110
Understand what's going on, identify
the opportunities in that data you've

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:43.040
understood, and then execute against it.

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:48.230
Another really interesting thing I think
was said earlier is data debt sucks and

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:49.800
you're going to regret it later.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.212
And your intuition is going
to run out at some point and

00:26:52.212 --> 00:26:53.611
then you're going to want data.

00:26:53.611 --> 00:26:54.364
In 2009,

00:26:54.364 --> 00:26:59.140
we actually shut down all of the work
the growth team was doing during January.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:01.000
And we only spent the time logging, and

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:03.740
making sure every critical
flow on Facebook.

00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:08.470
Registration, at the time we
didn't log clicks on emails.

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:11.950
So, click some emails, it seems
obvious now but we didn't have it.

00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:12.960
Clicks on the emails,

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:17.580
did you then actually log in or
did you fail in the password entry.

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:20.570
All of these different things,
we logged them so

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:22.060
that we could understand
what was going on.

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:25.235
And that was super critical because
actually in March that year,

00:27:25.235 --> 00:27:26.916
we had a massive growth slow down.

00:27:26.916 --> 00:27:30.866
And we would not have been able to
understand what was going on if we hadn't

00:27:30.866 --> 00:27:32.900
done the logging in January.

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:36.870
Because what went on was
actually super new ones and

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:38.710
without that logging in place,
we wouldn't have known.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:42.490
Get the data logged so
you can run through this process.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:48.260
So speaking of data, I am very,
very lucky at Facebook.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:52.992
I do growth marketing, I've run
the awesome internationalization team.

00:27:52.992 --> 00:27:55.061
But I also run the analytics team for
the company and

00:27:55.061 --> 00:27:56.420
that's been a couple years now.

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:00.430
When I talk about data
to people internally,

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:02.090
I like to say these three points.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:08.150
There's a meme in the valley that you
operate either for user experience or for

00:28:08.150 --> 00:28:09.500
metrics.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.590
And I really want to bust that meme.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:17.000
Data when used in the right way
gives you empathy about your users.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:22.790
One example I've talked about in
the past is we launched a product on

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:26.240
Facebook that you may have seen
on your big 42 inch monitors.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:31.520
No one has a big 42 inch monitor in here,
but I'm sure some of you do at home.

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:35.130
That has on the right-hand side all the
friends you can chat with on Facebook, but

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:37.990
at the top it has this list of stories
that's ticking over called ticker.

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:41.580
When you're on a small screen,

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:45.410
that product used to collapse into
the right-hand column of Facebook and

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:48.440
push things down so that you could
still see these stories ticking over.

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.000
That product worked great for
all of us who had

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:57.350
huge numbers of friends who are creating
a lot of content on big screens.

00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:02.480
But if you went to the Philippines where
there was huge usage in Internet cafes and

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.030
people run narrow screens,

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:07.466
the majority that amended up having
that in the right-hand column.

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:11.970
And the majority of them were
low engagement with Facebook and

00:29:11.970 --> 00:29:13.850
couldn't see people you
may know below the fold.

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:19.870
And so, we actually lost friending amongst
users who are very different to ourselves.

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:23.060
And we only figured out what was
happening by looking at the data and

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:27.150
saying, why is this bad on small
screens and awesome on big screens?

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:31.860
Why is it not so great for low engagement
users and super for power users?

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:36.020
It was because we used the data to
understand these users who are different

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:37.910
to us here in Silicon Valley.

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:39.860
Now think about it,
I presume I don't know.

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:42.240
I'm seeing a lot of Apple products here.

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.179
I have a Lenovo at the moment.

00:29:44.179 --> 00:29:46.730
But most of you probably have a iPhones.

00:29:46.730 --> 00:29:49.120
Who has a iPhone?

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:49.780
Who has an Android?

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:51.920
You're the best.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:55.040
Most people in the world
are using Androids.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.940
But everyone in this room has an iPhone,
first and foremost.

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:01.110
You don't understand.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:03.070
I'm actually forgetting
how to use iOS now,

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:04.400
which actually is a problem in my job.

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:08.750
But I've got an Android because
the majority, maybe not the majority,

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:13.210
a vast number of our users use Android,
as Jan said in the last talk.

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:17.400
And I need to understand what
the Android operating system offers.

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:20.929
But I can't understand Android and
iOS well as a user at the same time.

00:30:20.929 --> 00:30:24.120
So, I used data to understand
what's going on on iOS.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:27.220
You need to gain empathy for your users.

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:30.110
And research is great, and
talking to your customers is great.

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:35.807
But if you listen to the Twitch guy,

00:30:35.807 --> 00:30:43.160
Emmit, talk to your users then go away and
build the product to serve them.

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:44.761
And look at the data, in my opinion,

00:30:44.761 --> 00:30:47.563
would be the next thing to validate
if you built the right thing.

00:30:47.563 --> 00:30:49.361
If you're doing the right thing,

00:30:49.361 --> 00:30:53.100
if your research actually ties up with
what your users have said to you.

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:55.140
So, data gives you empathy.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:58.550
It's not a trade-off between metrics and
users.

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:02.817
If you build the right metrics,
you are optimizing for the users.

00:31:02.817 --> 00:31:04.940
Two, data predicts the future.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:07.880
Over and over and over again,
I love this quote.

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.430
It's a science fiction outright thing.

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:14.090
But like the future is here,
it's just not evenly distributed yet.

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:17.454
So, those curves that
show you the retention.

00:31:17.454 --> 00:31:20.205
When I said you can look at your
total addressable market and

00:31:20.205 --> 00:31:23.430
say, if I sign them all up,
how much revenue would I get?

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:25.080
Or you just look at who
you signed up today.

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:28.290
Let's say back in those days, we signed
up a thousand advertisers in a day.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:30.890
I could tell you how much
revenue they would contribute

00:31:30.890 --> 00:31:33.220
hundred days out because of that curve.

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:35.230
Now, also you're users
are going to ramp up.

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:39.390
So, a user who shops in one category
if you're eBay or if you're Amazon and

00:31:39.390 --> 00:31:40.840
then buys in a second category and

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:43.440
a third category is clearly
going to get more valuable, right?

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:48.030
Shopping the shop, driving up same store
sales is a common technique in e-commerce.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.260
Okay, so

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:52.950
how many of your users are increasing the
number of categories they're shopping in?

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:54.440
You see someone sign up.

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:58.400
What likelihood is it that they're
going to add extra categories, and

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.420
what is your revenue
going to be in the future?

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:01.980
You can predict that with data.

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:06.310
AB tests are another great thing.

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:10.020
See all these people walking in
the science march believing in science.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:13.310
And then when you apply the scientific
method to their product and

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:17.020
run an AB test, they ship the thing
that looked bad in the AB test

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:18.490
because they didn't believe the AB test.

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:22.669
I see that with all kinds
of startups I talk to.

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:30.943
AB tests tell you what's going to
happen when you ship them to 100%,

00:32:30.943 --> 00:32:32.226
use them it works.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:37.335
Finally, and this is something Mark
actually added recently for me.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:41.168
Data helps you make decisions faster.

00:32:44.003 --> 00:32:48.599
If you can not as the CEO, have to have
a conversation over something where you

00:32:48.599 --> 00:32:52.699
could just run a test, your company
is going to be able to move quicker,

00:32:52.699 --> 00:32:56.110
because every decision
won't have to come to you.

00:32:56.110 --> 00:32:57.036
People on the team,

00:32:57.036 --> 00:33:00.098
if they're clear on what that goal is,
that north star metric is.

00:33:00.098 --> 00:33:02.322
They can run the test,
see if they moved the metric and

00:33:02.322 --> 00:33:05.300
then only come to you when they know
if they've moved the metric or not.

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:09.361
Decisions like that help you move faster.

00:33:09.361 --> 00:33:12.660
Knowing what your most important priority
is because you've looked at the data.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:14.221
That helps you move faster.

00:33:16.332 --> 00:33:17.719
Make sense?

00:33:17.719 --> 00:33:18.219
Cool.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:23.670
So now you want me to push as much of
the time as possible so I'm pushing for

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:24.910
as much as time as possible.

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:26.510
So let's talk about tactics.

00:33:26.510 --> 00:33:27.850
First 100 users.

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:29.380
Sam talked about this brilliantly.

00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:32.500
If you actually listen I thought
Tracy in the previous conversation

00:33:32.500 --> 00:33:33.730
was just fantastic.

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:37.581
When she talked about getting 30 of her
friends in the construction industry to

00:33:37.581 --> 00:33:40.991
try Plangrid, and then asking them
to pay and 29 of them kept paying.

00:33:40.991 --> 00:33:44.056
Like ask your friends first for
your first 100 customers.

00:33:44.056 --> 00:33:46.883
Get people who will give
you honest feedback, and

00:33:46.883 --> 00:33:50.400
I love the point about make them pay,
if it's a paid product.

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:57.680
Second, research and then reach out to
who you'd like to have as your users.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.650
Totally makes sense, right?

00:33:59.650 --> 00:34:04.310
Like, you can do how Oscar was
discovered by the Lob Team right.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:07.364
Just email a bunch of people,
don't get caught in a spam filter.

00:34:07.364 --> 00:34:11.200
Don't just auto generate emails or you'll
definitely get caught in a spam filter.

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:12.690
But send personal emails,

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:15.340
get introductions,
see which friend you have in common.

00:34:16.340 --> 00:34:20.435
I emphasize by the way, I have never start
up, so I might be entirely the wrong

00:34:20.435 --> 00:34:23.912
person to tell you about first 100 users,
but Sam asked me to.

00:34:23.912 --> 00:34:26.760
Third, social media and
PR, PR is unscalable, but

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:28.780
it can give you amazing bumps.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:31.050
Amazing bumps.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:33.634
And in the early days I think
PR is a good growth leader.

00:34:33.634 --> 00:34:37.091
Later on, I think companies don't realize
when PRs stop being a growth lever,

00:34:37.091 --> 00:34:38.860
because they have these other metrics.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:41.070
But you can get these
great one-time bumps.

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:45.158
Just know you can't get them every single
week, and sometimes they are a one off.

00:34:45.158 --> 00:34:46.766
And finally, by ads.

00:34:46.766 --> 00:34:50.880
I am not as against ads as Sam is,
as a great method for growing users.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:53.600
I think that buying ads can
be absolutely fantastic.

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:57.312
I built my career buying ads, two of
the most valuable internet companies,

00:34:57.312 --> 00:35:00.618
Google and Facebook make all their
revenue from selling ads like ads

00:35:00.618 --> 00:35:01.958
are actually a good thing.

00:35:01.958 --> 00:35:04.260
If you get the targeting right and we're
going to talk about that in a second.

00:35:06.482 --> 00:35:10.330
If you're having a growth team, if you're
focused on growth tactics what people

00:35:10.330 --> 00:35:13.860
talk about is growth hacking,
though I hate the term If you pour fuel.

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:17.670
This is a picture from a guy
called Nick on my team, Nick.

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.510
I did not tell him to hold his hand into
the flames, which you'll see in a second.

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:26.803
If you pour lighter fluid onto dead
coals with no spark, you get nothing.

00:35:26.803 --> 00:35:29.621
And if there's one thing to
take away from this lecture,

00:35:29.621 --> 00:35:32.585
you need to have product
market fit to drive growth.

00:35:32.585 --> 00:35:34.902
You need retention to drive growth.

00:35:34.902 --> 00:35:36.965
Otherwise, every growth tactic,

00:35:36.965 --> 00:35:40.895
every acquisition tactic you could
possibly run doesn't matter.

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:45.661
But if you have a spark there.

00:35:45.661 --> 00:35:50.036
If you have a glowing ember you can pour
fuel on the fire of your product and

00:35:50.036 --> 00:35:52.790
that is what growth tactics does.

00:35:52.790 --> 00:35:55.240
So I look at things based on channel.

00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:57.740
Targeting creative and then conversion.

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:00.850
So you think about channel,
I'm going to do some greatest hits here.

00:36:00.850 --> 00:36:04.600
If ou look at SEO, you think,
I have to think about keyword research.

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:08.653
Which keywords do I want to show up for
on Google on the other search engines?

00:36:08.653 --> 00:36:11.610
And make sure you have content
targeted against those keywords.

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:14.150
You need to get links,
links from the internet to your site.

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:17.460
If you're a Silicon Valley startup you
technically get a lot of links off high

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:19.810
value sites so you usually
don't have to worry about that.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.610
But then you need to
link inside your site.

00:36:21.610 --> 00:36:23.850
Many people will tell you
links don't matter anymore,

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:26.140
in my experience that's not true.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:29.410
I don't know why it's not true, but
it's not been true in my experience.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:31.800
And then finally, there are a bunch
of basics out there on SEO,

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:32.660
you can look them up.

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:34.418
There are a load of websites
that talk about them.

00:36:34.418 --> 00:36:35.183
STO book,

00:36:35.183 --> 00:36:39.490
MOOs, there are a bunch of websites out
there you can read about SCO tactics.

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:40.657
Four page search.

00:36:40.657 --> 00:36:43.910
The biggest thing I see people do wrong
is they dont think about incrementality.

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:47.047
They by page search and
they don't necessarily say, well,

00:36:47.047 --> 00:36:49.211
would I have got those customers anyway?

00:36:49.211 --> 00:36:50.280
So look really hard.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:53.800
If you're going to buy page search as
a startup you should see a step change

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:54.910
in your line.

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:55.730
Your marketing team,

00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:58.600
if you've reached the stage where you have
a marketing guy should show you a line

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:02.140
that is flat and then they turn
on page search and it goes up.

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:04.980
That's the same of any ads
if you are a small startup.

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:08.907
If your a big company incrementality
analysis there's this great Berkeley

00:37:08.907 --> 00:37:12.831
professor Steve Tedelis whose done a deep
study in it with Ebay that I love and

00:37:12.831 --> 00:37:14.321
I think he's really smart.

00:37:14.321 --> 00:37:17.663
Big companies, you have to be very,
very smart about getting incrementally.

00:37:17.663 --> 00:37:18.700
You're a small startup.

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:21.733
Show me a step change.

00:37:21.733 --> 00:37:23.960
Do think about marginal not absolute ROI.

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:27.270
So people always look at I spent
this much I got this much.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:28.590
How valuable is it?

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:29.650
That's not what you want to look at.

00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:31.639
You want to look at
the last dollar you spent.

00:37:31.639 --> 00:37:32.920
How much did you get for that?

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:34.309
Not overall does that make sense?

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:37.527
Not as many knots.

00:37:37.527 --> 00:37:39.367
I'm going to blast through that.

00:37:39.367 --> 00:37:43.080
Look at margin ROI, look at diminishing
return curves and economics.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:47.560
And then again,
you need to do keyword research.

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:49.750
So email and SMS push notifications.

00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:51.990
This is something every
startup gets wrong.

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:53.870
We always optimize for ourselves.

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:58.250
Have you seen the Instagram thing on the
internet where someone shows a phone of

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:01.790
an Instagram user with a million
followers when they posted something and

00:38:01.790 --> 00:38:03.280
they have push notifications on.

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:04.904
And the string just goes [SOUND] and

00:38:04.904 --> 00:38:07.408
all the notifications
are coming in at high speed?

00:38:07.408 --> 00:38:10.450
That's what we're like for
our own products.

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:13.490
For our products we are the power
users and by nature

00:38:13.490 --> 00:38:17.940
every start up I have gone and talked
to has always optimized for themselves.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:21.001
Has always optimized for the power user.

00:38:21.001 --> 00:38:23.180
And so by optimizing for
the power user, what you do?

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:26.340
Well, we shouldn't put down by default
because it's going to completely expand my

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:26.840
whole screen.

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:29.610
Power users are smart.

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:33.158
If you give them the options,
they know how to turn off notifications.

00:38:33.158 --> 00:38:36.419
It's your marginal users you should
care about with notifications.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:42.890
That person for whom they received
their first Like on Instagram or

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:45.800
the host that gets their
first notification on Airbnb.

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:48.790
Or the seller that gets
their first bid on Ebay or

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:52.002
the buyer who's just been
out bid in their first item.

00:38:52.002 --> 00:38:55.172
That's the person that needs
to get the notification.

00:38:55.172 --> 00:38:59.100
And needs to respond to it, and
you need to optimize for in your head.

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:02.820
Marginal user is the number
one thing in email, SMS,

00:39:02.820 --> 00:39:06.730
push, notifications, and even offline
mail which Rob was talking about and

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:08.720
is still very important for
many companies.

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:11.760
People get wrong,
they optimize for themselves.

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:15.602
Beyond that you need to make sure it
gets delivered, opened, clicked on, etc.

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:21.498
There's so much more here and

00:39:21.498 --> 00:39:24.660
if I push through all of it I'm
not going to get to any questions.

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:27.420
Do you want me to get to questions?

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:28.480
&gt;&gt; This is pretty important.

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:29.760
How about this and no questions.

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.910
&gt;&gt; This and no questions,
is everybody okay with that?

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:33.100
Everybody for next week.

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:35.770
&gt;&gt; So think about on site merchandising.

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:36.920
You own a lot of media.

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:39.260
There's this idea of earned,
bought, and owned media.

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:40.427
Does that make sense?

00:39:40.427 --> 00:39:41.835
So if you post on Facebook and

00:39:41.835 --> 00:39:44.720
it gets loads of likes you've
earned that media, right.

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:48.918
If you bought an ad on Facebook or google
or somewhere, you've bought that media.

00:39:48.918 --> 00:39:52.331
And then if they're on your own website,
you own that media.

00:39:52.331 --> 00:39:56.214
People underestimate
the value of owned media.

00:39:56.214 --> 00:40:01.065
If you have a lot of traffic on your site,
ask them to invite their friends.

00:40:01.065 --> 00:40:04.710
Ask them to complete that
next action in your product.

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:08.750
Think about your on-site merchandising
as an extension of your product, but

00:40:08.750 --> 00:40:12.770
understand that on-site merchandising
is a marketing problem.

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:16.430
So how can you trigger the right thing?

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:20.640
Someone has just bought an item for
you, immediately like Amazon does.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:24.190
Trigger for them a set of suggestions
like why don't you buy this and this, so

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:27.950
the customers who bought this also
bought this, works incredibly well.

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:31.250
What about creative?

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.590
Creative is really important.

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:39.030
It should be personalized, because they're
on your site, you know their name.

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:43.170
Hey Alex, why don't you buy these items
that other similar customers have bought.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:47.840
Performs way better than buy these other
items that similar customers have bought.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:50.020
That's one simple personalization.

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:52.690
But instead of just saying this
customer bought this item,

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:53.960
you should do a prediction.

00:40:53.960 --> 00:40:57.900
This person, all the items they've
ever bought let me predict for

00:40:57.900 --> 00:41:01.220
them in a personalized way
what item they should buy.

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:04.940
Look at Facebook and how often we say
five of your friends have done x.

00:41:04.940 --> 00:41:07.520
Social context, that's personalization.

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.930
Use people's own profile picture.

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:15.230
We have a great personalization example
where when you're on a Facebook page,

00:41:15.230 --> 00:41:18.670
we show you what your ad would
look like if you bought it,

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:20.620
with a Buy This button underneath it.

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:22.305
It could not be more personalized.

00:41:22.305 --> 00:41:26.460
On-site merchandising gives you so
many opportunities to deeply,

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:28.900
deeply personalize the creative.

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:31.220
And finally, paid and organic social.

00:41:31.220 --> 00:41:34.210
I do work for Facebook,
we do make all of are money from ads.

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:38.620
And I personally think that I am not the
best person to speak about buying ads on

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:41.900
Facebook, because I'm the guy who buys ads
off Facebook to drive traffic to Facebook.

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:45.620
But what I'll say is if I left
Facebook today, and I was a start-up

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:49.540
I would be crazy not to use our custom
audiences and look-alike targeting.

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:53.090
So I highly, highly encourage you if you
think about nothing else on Facebook ads

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:53.910
to look into those.

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:57.490
It's a great way for
you to say these are my best customers.

00:41:57.490 --> 00:41:59.020
Upload the list to Facebook.

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.070
How do I find more customers like those?

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:03.790
It works incredibly well for
a lot of companies.

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.960
And the other thing across all social
media is to a great extent you're

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:08.228
dealing with interesting squared.

00:42:08.228 --> 00:42:11.950
Because the click-through rate
on your thing, the like rate or

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:14.670
whatever on your thing will be
related to how interesting it is, and

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:18.540
also the distribution, whether it's being
shared via a messaging app, reshared on

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:22.440
Twitter, or on Facebook, will be driven
by whether people found it interesting.

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:22.940
Make sense?

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:24.760
Cool.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:27.260
Okay, targeting.

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:28.560
Behavioral is most important.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:34.250
So what you did Is much more
important than who you are.

00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:36.890
There are 50 year old,
60 year old parents and

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:39.960
grandparents who don't need two
friends to be active on Facebook.

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:41.030
And there are some who need 500.

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:45.020
And there are teenagers who need
500 friends and some who need two,

00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:49.100
and you're much better to figure out like
what is the behavior that tells you that.

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:53.110
Then say on a demographic basis,
I'm going to do this versus this.

00:42:53.110 --> 00:42:58.140
People who buying Nike's, Men's Nike
trainers typically are men, but sometimes

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:02.780
people buy for their boyfriend, or
their husband, or their dad or whatever.

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:05.830
And so if you just target women
with women's trainers and

00:43:05.830 --> 00:43:08.020
men with men's trainers
you are making a mistake.

00:43:08.020 --> 00:43:12.730
What you should do is look at what
signals you to show the right ad or

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:15.660
the right content to the right
person at the right time.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:16.273
Targeting matters.

00:43:19.077 --> 00:43:20.900
I've talked about this before.

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:22.470
Okay, creative.

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:23.820
Creative is important but

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:28.010
it's nowhere near as important as the
above two unless you have a 10x creative.

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:30.450
If you produce
the Dollar Shave Club video,

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:33.800
probably it's very very worth it but
most people don't produce

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:36.720
a piece of creative that
amazing that changes the world.

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:41.790
Typically I find targeting comes before
creative in every single way, and

00:43:41.790 --> 00:43:42.960
channel comes before that.

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:44.080
Does that make sense?

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:47.930
So creative is awesome and really
important and can give you a big uplift,

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:51.500
but think about your channel first and
your targeting second.

00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:53.460
If you have creative though,
you should put it in context,

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:55.870
you should personalize it,
you give it a call to action.

00:43:55.870 --> 00:43:58.430
On call to action,
this is a great example.

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:02.190
Where on every ad back in the day on
Facebook we had a little link at the top

00:44:02.190 --> 00:44:03.260
that said advertise.

00:44:04.670 --> 00:44:07.760
We translated it wrong
by mistake in French.

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:11.370
We found this with the data in French,
we translated to clear an ad.

00:44:12.565 --> 00:44:13.885
Create an ad.

00:44:13.885 --> 00:44:15.285
And we had significantly more,

00:44:15.285 --> 00:44:17.955
actually 40% more acquisition
from that channel that

00:44:17.955 --> 00:44:21.445
was at the time our single most important
channel for advertiser acquisition.

00:44:21.445 --> 00:44:22.845
We took the French translation and

00:44:22.845 --> 00:44:25.110
we reverse translated it and
changed the whole world.

00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:28.990
Instead of saying the kind of passive
advertise to the active create an ad.

00:44:28.990 --> 00:44:33.020
And we achieved a 40% bump in that
most important channel for us for

00:44:33.020 --> 00:44:34.850
advertiser acquisition.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:38.010
That is the call to action,
like what you say matters.

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:40.610
So when I say creative doesn't
matter as much as channel, and

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:43.390
targeting, it still matters a lot.

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:45.135
And the words you use matter a lot.

00:44:47.464 --> 00:44:50.560
This is the example I said
about in context with intent.

00:44:50.560 --> 00:44:54.370
If you are on your own page,
this used to be ad we would show to you,

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:57.610
that say hey this is what your ad
would look like elsewhere on the site.

00:44:57.610 --> 00:45:01.600
And you can see that by hour this
was the hour that we doubled

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:05.950
advertiser acquisition for
Facebook with the weekly push.

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:09.030
If you want to see more about this, Brian
Hale does a great talk about this at F8,

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:10.320
and it's online.

00:45:10.320 --> 00:45:14.090
And then the last thing, yes.

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:15.330
The last thing is conversion.

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:18.480
Conversion is really really important, but

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:21.310
it's not important until you've thought
about all the other things first.

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:23.590
So things to think about with conversion.

00:45:23.590 --> 00:45:26.370
The landing page people land on
will determine what your conversion

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:28.130
rate is going to be.

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:32.010
If you can, don't have one button
on it that they then have to click

00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:33.850
to go to the registration form.

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:36.180
Put the registration
form in the landing page.

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:40.700
It's important to know what your
most important metric is though.

00:45:40.700 --> 00:45:44.470
Back at eBay, what we had was this
original metric confirmed registered

00:45:44.470 --> 00:45:48.750
users, and a new metric activated
confirmed registered users.

00:45:48.750 --> 00:45:52.670
And an activated confirmed registered
user was someone who bought an item,

00:45:52.670 --> 00:45:55.000
bid on an item, listed an item.

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:58.910
And a confirmed registered user was
someone who registered and confirmed and

00:45:58.910 --> 00:46:00.170
that was it.

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:03.730
To get confirmed registered users we
wanted to pump all of our search traffic,

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:06.680
this was back in 2004 we were
spending huge amounts on search.

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:09.220
We wanted to pump all of our search
traffic at the registration page.

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:14.200
To get activated confirmed registered
users we actually wanted to land it

00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:16.760
on the search term that
you were looking for.

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:17.530
So if you were looking for

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:20.810
a trampoline, show you the trampolines
you could buy on eBay.

00:46:22.330 --> 00:46:22.910
That change,

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:27.180
and we changed all of our advertising
to change the landing page, dropped

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:30.860
confirmed registered users significantly
that we were acquiring for eBay.

00:46:31.930 --> 00:46:36.180
But it increased activated
confirmed registered users.

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:40.330
It increased revenue and
lifetime value per registered user.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:44.630
And it increased total revenue per day
from the people we were acquiring.

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:49.840
So although I think you can focus on
the registration form really hard.

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:51.370
Any commas, sometimes it's really,

00:46:51.370 --> 00:46:54.330
really important to land people on
what they are searching for, so

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:57.000
that they will activate and
they will buy on your service.

00:46:57.000 --> 00:47:00.010
So it's not as simple as just put
the registration form online.

00:47:01.580 --> 00:47:02.730
But we did do that on Facebook.

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:07.710
So the final thing I like to
say is Facebook moves fast.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:11.660
And move fast is an absolute
core value for us as a company.

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:15.406
Like core, core, core value and we still
do bold things like taking messenger out

00:47:15.406 --> 00:47:17.650
of Facebook,
that was challenging at the time.

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:21.280
But I think now people are seeing
how valuable a decision that was.

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:24.540
And that was a project that I was
deeply involved with and ran.

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:29.290
We're willing to move fast, we're willing
to be bold because fundamentally a good

00:47:29.290 --> 00:47:35.620
plan violently executed on today is
better than a perfect plan next week.

00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:37.220
Data helps you move fast.

00:47:37.220 --> 00:47:39.125
These growth tactics help you move fast.

00:47:39.125 --> 00:47:43.510
Best of talks about making decisions with
70% of the data not 90% of the data.

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:45.085
And I deeply believe in that.

00:47:45.085 --> 00:47:49.780
So move fast, focus on retention and

00:47:49.780 --> 00:47:55.080
have one key metric
that you care about for

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:58.428
your company's growth.

00:47:58.428 --> 00:47:59.139
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:47:59.139 --> 00:48:00.255
&gt;&gt; Okay, apparently we have time for

00:48:00.255 --> 00:48:00.760
one question.

00:48:00.760 --> 00:48:01.368
I'm sorry.

00:48:01.368 --> 00:48:02.933
Go for it.

00:48:02.933 --> 00:48:08.321
&gt;&gt; So you mentioned that [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:08.321 --> 00:48:11.311
How do you think that creating the.

00:48:11.311 --> 00:48:14.518
&gt;&gt; So the question was for the magic
moments, you can kind of tell how you can

00:48:14.518 --> 00:48:18.220
figure out went they happened and
that how you can create them.

00:48:18.220 --> 00:48:21.620
I mean honestly if you look at LinkedIn,

00:48:21.620 --> 00:48:24.330
Facebook, Twitter every social
network in the known universe.

00:48:24.330 --> 00:48:27.540
What we do is, we immediately
ask you to import your contacts.

00:48:27.540 --> 00:48:30.710
So once we know that friends is
the thing that we need to do for

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:34.580
you, we focus on what is the way to
remove friction from getting you friends.

00:48:34.580 --> 00:48:38.070
If you look at the example I used with
eBay, we hadn't really figured out that

00:48:38.070 --> 00:48:41.970
activating you was important to
generating the revenue long term.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:45.590
So we shifted the landing page and the
registration form to the search results.

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:49.210
So we got you to the item you wanted
to buy as quickly as possible.

00:48:49.210 --> 00:48:51.710
And that caused you to activate and
get to that magic moment faster.

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:54.650
So those are the kind of
things once you identify it

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:57.580
I actually think the tactics
fall out very logically.

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:01.030
My boss Javi likes to say that common
sense is the least common of all senses

00:49:01.030 --> 00:49:02.280
and I agree with him.

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:05.934
But if you step back and just look okay,
if I want more people to buy an item on

00:49:05.934 --> 00:49:08.371
eBay should I land them on
the registration page or

00:49:08.371 --> 00:49:09.664
the search results page?

00:49:09.664 --> 00:49:12.070
You'd probably land them on
the search result page and

00:49:12.070 --> 00:49:14.231
that would get them to
that magic moment faster.

00:49:14.231 --> 00:49:14.974
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:49:14.974 --> 00:49:15.825
&gt;&gt; Thank you, okay.

00:49:15.825 --> 00:49:16.810
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

